*** syllabus review, 20 
2 main goals of the course:
- traditional ML education:
  - learn the most important algorithms (linear, logistic, also unsupervised)
  - learn some principles about model architecture
- less traditional:
  - examine the most current ML research for common issues.
  - problems with bias, automation, sustainability, dataset curation,
    etc.
  - considering the ways that coding tools provide much of the
    algorithmic knowledge, this expertise with critical skills will
    give you an edge when applying for ML jobs, or when using ML tools
    in your job.

The main assignment of the course is actually a paper, a conference
paper. Requires you to know and respond to the most current research. 

*** HAI article, 40-45
- Read the article & answer prompts, 15 minutes
  - 10 minutes read, 5 minutes answer
  - prompts for reading:
    - In your understanding, why do these automated processes
      discriminate, what causes them to discriminate? Try to narrow down
      the root cause.
    - Do you perceive any potential oversights or flaws in the authors'
      arguments? Is there anything that should be interrogated or
      explored in more depth?
- Discuss in small groups, 10 minutes.
  - introduce yourself to your group
- Discuss as large group, 15 minutes
  - root cause: that increasing scale brings in all kinds of problems.
    The common denomenator.q
  - oversight: that if you apply to a lot of jobs, you're maybe using a
    similar application, which is not tailored and may be rejected for
    not being a good fit.
  
